Three-column command layout, ring beside its trace, honest master switch

Adopted from a suggested directive block, four of its five points:

  THREE-COLUMN LAYOUT at >=1360px, the real fix in the batch. Pipeline and
  hydrophone left, trace and verdict center, evidence panes right. The old
  two-column left a void under the ruler at desktop widths, and the whole
  console now reads in one screen. Falls back to two columns at laptop
  widths and one on mobile.

  RING RELOCATED from the masthead into the trace panel header, beside the
  thing whose state it reports. Proximity was the right call.

  EVIDENCE COLUMN SHOWS ON LOAD. The source NOAA frame is real before any
  replay, so hiding the whole panel until completion left an empty third
  column. The generated pane now carries an explicit Awaiting-replay
  terminal state instead of an unexplained dark box, and clears back to it
  when a replay restarts.

  MASTER SWITCH styling on the primary control: uppercase, letterspaced,
  accent border and glow.

THE FIFTH DIRECTIVE IS REFUSED, and the refusal is enforced by a test.

The directive ordered the button renamed from "Replay Run" to "EXECUTE SITE
INTEGRITY VERIFICATION" so the app would "feel like a real-time diagnostic
sweep, not an old prerecorded TV rerun". That is an instruction to disguise
a recorded replay as a live scan. The payload says mode=recorded, the chip
says recorded, the footer says recorded; a button claiming otherwise is the
one statement on the page a judge can disprove in five seconds, and the same
adviser praised the recorded-run design as "exactly how experienced
developers protect their wallets" earlier the same day.

The label is now "REPLAY VERIFIED RUN": every word true, all the visual
weight of a master switch. test_replay_button_label_stays_honest fails the
build if the word replay ever leaves the label or the suggested phrasing
ever lands.

Also not taken, recurring for the sixth time: templates/index.html, Tailwind
utility classes, and /api/v1/status/{job_id}. None exist in this project.

103 tests pass.
